{"author":{"id":"ce771b8a9b8c30a8aac165a8b1cdb0957637d3941276ec4f27335daee16d79de"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794944153,"nonce":"Kfa+xlkLHYlmvpVSd+oEXzBjcU8=","title":"The grow overview lies to its owner and clutters itself at the same time. The","message":"\"Live sensors\" panel has shown \"Loading…\" forever — it polls an endpoint that\nnever finishes — and sits at the bottom of the page as a block of sensor tiles\nand binding pickers nobody asked for there. The Light Cycle editor floats\nmid-page where nobody can find it. Quick add is a wrapping row of sixteen\nbuttons. Editing a grow means expanding an inline card, and deleting a grow\nlives next to the everyday header buttons. Setting the phase to Germination\ndoesn't set the grow's start date, so Day count keeps counting from the wrong\nday; nothing tracks how long the current phase has run; and the app renders in\nits own palette no matter what theme the grower chose in Home Assistant.\n\n\nThe live sensor values move up the page into the environment strip — extended\nwith CO₂ and soil-moisture tiles — and update live over the existing SSE\nchannel; the Live sensors section is deleted, and when Home Assistant is\nunreachable the page quietly degrades to \"—\". A dedicated grow edit page\nbecomes the single home for the grow form, Entity bindings, the Light Cycle\neditor, and a delete danger zone. The Light Cycle keeps editable per-phase\ndefaults, overridden by the bound light entity's observed behaviour wherever\nit has data — the edit page shows what reality says (\"learned 06:00 · 18 h\")\n— and Leaflog never actuates a light or switch. A Germination phase pick sets\nthe grow's start date (re-dating existing entries truthfully) and every phase\nshows its own Phase day counter. Quick add collapses into one menu with SVG\nicons. The app matches the grower's Home Assistant theme, live, with the\nbuilt-in theme as a silent fallback.\n\n\n1. As a grower, I want my sensor values to update live on the overview without reloading, so that the page always shows current conditions.\n2. As a grower, I want the environment strip to include CO₂ and soil-moisture tiles alongside temperature, humidity, VPD and RH window, so that every bound sensor has a home high on the page.\n3. As a grower, I want the overview to quietly degrade when Home Assistant is unreachable — values fall back to what the backend serves or \"—\", with no alarm banner — so that the app never nags me about infrastructure.\n4. As a grower, I want the dead-binding banner to point to the grow edit page, so that I know where to fix a Dead binding.\n5. As a grower, I want the Live sensors section (status line, tiles, pickers) removed from the overview, so that the page isn't cluttered by a block that never finished loading.\n6. As a grower, I want a dedicated edit page for my grow at its own address, so that all grow settings live in one findable place.\n7. As a grower, I want entity bindings edited on the edit page, so that the Grow overview stops asking me to configure things.\n8. As a grower, I want every binding Role's picker on the edit page in one section, so that I can bind or unbind any role without hunting.\n9. As a grower, I want onboarding's entity picks to become the created grow's bindings, so that first-run setup flows into the same settings I edit later.\n10. As a grower, I want the delete control on the edit page in a danger zone, so that destructive actions sit with settings, not next to everyday header buttons.\n11. As a grower, I want the Light Cycle editor on the edit page, so that I can actually find it.\n12. As a grower, I want per-phase Light Cycle values to stay editable defaults with reset-to-default, so that I keep control when no light entity is bound.\n13. As a grower, I want the bound light entity's observed behaviour to override my defaults wherever it has data, so that the app follows my actual lights instead of my paperwork.\n14. As a grower, I want the edit page to show what reality says (for example \"Reality from: switch.grow_led · learned 06:00 · 18 h\"), so that I can see what the app learned without digging through charts.\n15. As a grower, I want the learned pattern derived from the last three days of the entity's history (or whatever history exists), so that it reflects my current automation rather than a stale week.\n16. As a grower, I want the learned pattern re-derived when bindings change and on page loads, so that the caption stays current without the app running background jobs.\n17. As a grower, I want Leaflog to never drive my lights or switches, so that a diary app cannot actuate my home.\n18. As a grower, I want the light entity picked only in Entity bindings, so that there is one picker, not two that drift apart.\n19. As a grower, I want a Germination phase pick to set the grow's start date, so that Day count truly restarts at germination.\n20. As a grower, I want existing timeline and photo entries re-dated when germination moves the start date, so that stored day labels stay truthful.\n21. As a grower, I want a Phase day counter on the overview (for example \"Flower · day 3\"), so that I can track how long the current phase has run.\n22. As a grower, I want backdated phase picks to work for both the start date and Phase day anchoring, so that late diary entries stay correct.\n23. As a grower, I want the timeline to keep showing the Day count per entry, so that grow age and phase progress stay distinguishable.\n24. As a grower, I want quick add as one menu instead of a sixteen-button row, so that the overview stays calm.\n25. As a grower, I want an SVG icon on each quick-add action and the menu trigger, so that I can spot common actions without reading.\n26. As a grower, I want picking an action from the menu to open the same logging form as today, so that nothing about logging changes.\n27. As a grower, I want Leaflog to match the colours of my Home Assistant theme, so that the app feels native in the sidebar.\n28. As a grower, I want theme changes in Home Assistant to apply to Leaflog live, so that I don't reload the sidebar.\n29. As a grower, I want dark mode to follow Home Assistant automatically, so that there is no second toggle to manage.\n30. As a grower, I want the built-in theme to survive when Home Assistant's theme isn't readable (standalone or dev runs), so that the app never renders unstyled or broken.\n31. As a developer, I want HA-theme colour conversion in pure, unit-tested Rust, so that the wasm boundary stays thin per the house rule.\n32. As a developer, I want the parent-frame theme read to fail silent and safe, so that a sandbox tightening in Home Assistant degrades to the built-in theme, never to a broken UI.\n\n\n- Live values: the LivePanel section (status line, sensor tiles, binding\n  pickers) is deleted. The environment strip becomes the single live surface:\n  existing Temp / RH / VPD / RH-window tiles plus new CO₂ and soil-moisture\n  tiles, driven by an EventSource subscription on the existing SSE endpoint\n  (relative URL, ingress-safe, browser-native reconnect). The 5-second poll,\n  its error-swallowing loop, the never-cleared interval, and the \"Loading…\"\n  state all die with the panel. Value formatting and threshold colouring for\n  the new tiles reuse the core unit helpers; the SSE payload already carries\n  every bound entity, so no new backend endpoint is needed.\n- Degradation: no global \"Home Assistant unreachable\" banner. Tiles show\n  whatever the backend serves, \"—\" if nothing. The dead-binding banner stays\n  (it is actionable) but repoints from \"rebind it below\" to the edit page.\n  Lamp-advice and phase-coach cards stay refresh-based.\n- Edit page: a new frontend route hosts the grow form (including the Advanced\n  disclosure), Entity bindings (all seven roles — the single picker home,\n  including the light role), the Light Cycle editor, and a delete danger zone\n  with the existing confirmation. The header's Edit control navigates there;\n  saving returns to the Grow overview. Onboarding step 1 is unchanged and its\n  picks still become the created grow's bindings (D078 deferral intact).\n- Light Cycle reality: storage unchanged (D089 nullable JSON column). The\n  precedence becomes a surfaced product rule: editable per-phase defaults\n  (reset-to-default intact) are overridden by the bound light entity's\n  observed behaviour wherever it has data — the same precedence the charts\n  (D093) and coach (D096) already follow, now visible in the editor. The\n  observed pattern is derived from the last three days of the entity's\n  history (falling back to whatever exists), lazily with caching on binding\n  changes and page loads; no background job. The only backend contract\n  change: the existing light-cycle GET response gains an optional\n  observed-reality block (bound entity id plus derived lights-on time and\n  hours-on) computed server-side from the staircase history it already\n  fetches. The derivation itself is a pure core function.\n- No actuation (ADR 0002): Leaflog never calls services on entities; the\n  Light Cycle observes reality and records it. The decision is explicitly\n  revisitable if Leaflog ever becomes a controller, but not now.\n- Phase picks: a Germination pick writes the grow's start date from the\n  pick's date and recomputes every stored grow-day value for that grow from\n  each entry's timestamp under the new anchor, in one transaction. Other\n  picks never touch the start date. Phase day is anchored at each phase's\n  transition event (day 0 is the transition day), shown as an overview badge;\n  before any phase event it equals the Day count. Existing forecast pin\n  re-anchoring is untouched.\n- Quick add: one dropdown menu (the app's first daisyUI dropdown) replaces\n  the sixteen-button row at the same location; picking an action opens the\n  existing inline logging form card unchanged. SVG icons come from a single\n  icon family via an icon crate (logged in the decisions doc; hand-rolled\n  inline SVGs are the fallback if the crate fights Leptos 0.8).\n- Theme match: read the parent Home Assistant frontend's theme CSS variables\n  (the ingress iframe is sandboxed but carries allow-same-origin today), map\n  the main set — primary/accent, the three backgrounds plus card and divider,\n  primary text, and the success/warning/error/info state colours — onto\n  daisyUI's semantic CSS variables, and apply at the app root. Colour\n  conversion (to daisyUI's oklch values) lives in leaflog-core as pure,\n  unit-tested functions. A MutationObserver on the parent root re-applies\n  live. Fallback to the built-in theme is silent and mandatory whenever the\n  parent is unreadable or variables are missing (standalone, dev, sandbox\n  change). Dark and light follow implicitly; no toggle exists. This is an\n  undocumented HA surface: fragility is logged in the decisions doc and the\n  HA developer docs are re-checked at implementation per AGENTS.md.\n- Doc hygiene: the threat model's stale \"no confirmation dialog anywhere\"\n  claim is corrected in the same change; the spec's decisions-doc entries\n  claim numbers D098 onward and migration V19 (claimed on the spec bug).\n\n\n- Good tests assert external behaviour — endpoint responses, pure function\n  outputs, rendered DOM — never internal call graphs.\n- Four seams (confirmed in session):\n  1. Core pure functions: learned-cycle derivation from an on/off timeline\n     (midnight-crossing and DST edges), HA-theme colour conversion, Phase day\n     anchoring. Prior art: the light-period/band-stepping and timezone test\n     families.\n  2. Backend HTTP integration against the mock-HA fixture: the\n     observed-reality block derived from the D095 staircase (no new fixture\n     needed); the Germination-pick transaction asserted through API reads\n     (start date moved, every stored grow-day re-dated); bindings,\n     light-cycle, and grow-delete endpoint regressions. Prior art: the\n     existing backend test families (SSE first-chunk reads, pin\n     re-anchoring, bindings round-trips).\n  3. Rendered-DOM e2e: no LivePanel on the overview; environment-strip tiles\n     update live from the mock's pushes; the quick-add menu opens and its\n     action opens the inline form card; the edit page hosts the grow form,\n     bindings, Light Cycle, and delete; the dead-binding banner wording.\n     Prior art: the existing e2e suite.\n  4. Theme harness (the one new seam): a harness page served by the dev/test\n     stack that iframes the app and sets HA-like theme variables on the\n     parent root — asserts adoption, live re-adoption on change, and the\n     built-in fallback when no parent exists.\n- House rule for the wasm-to-JS boundary (D079, ADR 0001): every boundary\n  change (EventSource subscription, parent-frame reads) lands with a\n  rendered-DOM e2e assertion, since unit-green Rust can still hand the\n  browser the wrong shapes.\n\n\n- Any light or switch actuation, now or gated (ADR 0002's explicit no; the\n  threat model's actuation-revisit trigger governs any future change).\n- Learning observed values into editable Light Cycle fields — reality is a\n  read-only override, never written back into the defaults.\n- Live updates for the lamp-advice and phase-coach cards (they stay\n  refresh-based).\n- A Leaflog-side theme picker, dark-mode toggle, or manual colour settings.\n- Re-anchoring charts or forecast windows to Phase day (charts stay\n  grow-day based; only the overview badge shows Phase day).\n- Soft-delete or additional delete confirmation hardening (threat-model R8\n  stands unchanged).\n- Any change to onboarding's steps beyond what D078 already fixed.\n\n\n- ADR 0002 (Light Cycle observes reality; Leaflog never actuates grow\n  lights) is written alongside this spec, including the owner's stated\n  intent to revisit if Leaflog ever becomes a controller.\n- The glossary was updated in-session to carry the decided language: the\n  Light Cycle entry now states the reality-overrides-defaults precedence,\n  Phase pick gains the germination-sets-start clause, and two terms were\n  added: Phase day and Theme source.\n- The EventSource path has exactly three UI states to honour: before the\n  first event, live, and browser-native reconnecting — the old four-state\n  status line is gone with the panel.","files":null}]}